V(PAC, IO_A,
  "A 52-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a rhythm strip in which a normal beat is followed by an early beat with an abnormal P wave and a narrow QRS, repeating so that every other beat is early (bigeminy). Which is the most likely diagnosis?",
  [["Premature atrial beats in bigeminy",
    "Correct. Early beats with an abnormal P wave and narrow QRS are premature atrial contractions (common in chronic lung disease), and an ectopic beat every other beat is bigeminy."],
   ["Ventricular bigeminy",
    "Premature ventricular contractions begin in the ventricles and are early, wide and bizarre with no P wave. Her early beats are narrow and each has an abnormal P wave, which places them in the atria. Tell: a narrow early beat with a P wave is atrial; a wide, bizarre early beat without one is ventricular.", "ruled out by a stated finding"],
   ["Flutter with 2:1 block",
    "Atrial flutter with 2:1 block shows a continuous sawtooth line at an atrial rate of 250 or more, with two flutter waves per QRS and a regular rhythm. Her strip alternates a normal beat with an early beat, each with a single P wave. Tell: flutter has no normal beats in between; bigeminy alternates normal and early beats.", "competing diagnosis"],
   ["Junctional rhythm",
    "A junctional rhythm is a regular, steady rhythm usually at 40 to 60 with P waves that are inverted, buried in the QRS or after it. This strip alternates normal beats with early beats that carry their own abnormal P wave. Tell: early beats point to ectopics; a steady slow rhythm with inverted or hidden P waves is junctional.", "competing diagnosis"]],
  C(54), "test finding", "diagnosis"),

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- premature junctional contractions and junctional rhythm (IO_L30)
V(JUN, IO_L30,
  "An 82-year-old woman taking digoxin for heart failure has nausea and fatigue. Her heart rate is 48 and regular, with narrow QRS complexes and retrograde P waves (inverted) just after each QRS in II, III and aVF. Which is the most likely cause of this rhythm?",
  [["Digoxin toxicity",
    "Correct. A slow, regular, narrow rhythm at 48 with inverted P waves just after each QRS in II, III and aVF is a junctional rhythm, and digoxin toxicity is a listed cause. Her nausea and fatigue while taking digoxin point to toxicity, which is treated with atropine and/or digoxin immune Fab (fragment antigen-binding)."],
   ["Hyperthyroidism",
    "Hyperthyroidism is a listed cause of sinus tachycardia, a fast rhythm with a P wave before each QRS. Her rhythm is slow at 48 with retrograde P waves after the QRS, a junctional rhythm. Tell: a slow junctional rhythm in a patient on digoxin points to digoxin toxicity, not the thyroid.", "reversed direction or value"],
   ["An accessory pathway",
    "An accessory pathway (bundle of Kent) shows as pre-excitation, a short PR with a delta wave, and allows fast reentrant tachycardia. Her rhythm is slow and regular at 48 with retrograde P waves, a junctional rhythm. Tell: an accessory pathway goes with fast rhythms and delta waves, not a slow junctional rhythm.", "adjacent fact"],
   ["Missed digoxin doses",
    "Digoxin toxicity, not missed doses, is the listed digoxin-related cause of junctional rhythm, and digoxin is also a listed cause of sinus bradycardia. A slow junctional rhythm with nausea and fatigue in a patient on digoxin points to too much drug, not too little. Tell: digoxin excess slows the heart; missing doses would not.", "reversed direction or value"]],
  C(64), "etiology", "diagnosis"),

V(JUN, IO_L30,
  "A 75-year-old man has a junctional rhythm at 42 with dizziness. Monitoring shows that the cause is high-grade atrioventricular block. Which treatment is indicated?",
  [["Permanent pacemaker",
    "Correct. A junctional rhythm at 42 with dizziness, caused by high-grade atrioventricular block, needs a permanent pacemaker. Management of junctional rhythm depends on the cause, and complete or high-grade block or sick sinus syndrome is the setting for a pacemaker."],
   ["Observation",
    "No treatment suits a junctional rhythm in an asymptomatic, otherwise healthy person, such as an athlete with high vagal tone. He is dizzy and has high-grade atrioventricular block behind it. Tell: block as the cause means a permanent pacemaker, not observation.", "wrong stage or severity"],
   ["Adenosine",
    "Adenosine is used in stable supraventricular tachycardia to convert the rhythm or slow it enough to reveal P waves. His rhythm is already slow at 42 from high-grade block, so slowing it further is the opposite of the goal. Tell: block plus a slow junctional rhythm calls for a pacemaker.", "confused mechanism or drug class"],
   ["Catheter ablation",
    "Catheter ablation is used for tachycardias, such as focal atrial tachycardia, nodal reentry or an accessory pathway. A slow junctional rhythm from high-grade block needs a permanent pacemaker. Tell: ablation removes fast circuits; it does not fix block.", "confused mechanism or drug class"]],
  C(64), "escalation", "treatment"),

V(JUN, IO_L30,
  "A 23-year-old competitive cyclist has a regular rhythm at 50 with narrow QRS complexes and no visible P waves on a screening electrocardiogram. He has no symptoms, and his echocardiogram is normal. Which is the most appropriate management?",
  [["No treatment",
    "Correct. A regular, narrow-complex rhythm at 50 with no visible P waves is a junctional rhythm, usually 40 to 60, and it can be seen in healthy people with increased vagal tone, such as well-conditioned athletes. With no symptoms and a normal echocardiogram in an otherwise healthy cyclist, no treatment is needed."],
   ["Permanent pacemaker",
    "A permanent pacemaker is for a junctional rhythm caused by complete or high-grade atrioventricular block or by sick sinus syndrome. This cyclist's rhythm comes from high vagal tone in a normal heart and causes no symptoms. Tell: management depends on the cause, and a healthy, asymptomatic athlete needs none.", "wrong stage or severity"],
   ["Atropine",
    "Atropine is used when a junctional rhythm comes from digitalis toxicity, and as first-line treatment for acute, unstable bradycardia. This cyclist has no symptoms, no drug exposure and a normal heart. Tell: atropine needs a cause like digoxin toxicity or instability, and a healthy athlete has neither.", "wrong stage or severity"],
   ["Stop training",
    "His training is the likely reason for the rhythm, since increased vagal tone in well-conditioned athletes is a recognized cause of junctional rhythm. Junctional rhythm is generally well tolerated and needs no treatment when asymptomatic in an otherwise healthy person. Tell: an asymptomatic athlete with a normal heart needs no restriction.", "common lay belief"]],
  C(64), "first-line", "next step"),

V(JUN, IO_L30,
  "A 64-year-old man is admitted with an inferior myocardial infarction. His heart rate falls to 46 with narrow QRS complexes and P waves that are either inverted (retrograde P waves) or buried in the QRS. Which rhythm is most likely?",
  [["Junctional rhythm",
    "Correct. A regular, narrow-complex rhythm at 46 with P waves inverted or buried in the QRS means the impulse starts in the atrioventricular (AV) node: a junctional rhythm, usually 40 to 60. When the low node fires first the P wave is retrograde, after the QRS; mid-node firing buries it. Inferior myocardial infarction is a listed cause."],
   ["Sinus bradycardia",
    "Sinus bradycardia fits the slow rate, but the impulse starts in the sinus node, so every QRS has a uniform P wave before it with a PR of 0.12 to 0.20 seconds. P waves that are inverted after the QRS or buried in it do not fit. Tell: same slow rate, but a junctional rhythm loses the normal P wave and PR interval.", "ruled out by a stated finding"],
   ["Atrial flutter",
    "Atrial flutter fills the baseline with sawtooth flutter waves at an atrial rate of 250 or more, outnumbering the QRS complexes. This man has a slow rate of 46 and only one P wave per beat, inverted or buried in the QRS. Tell: sawtooth waves mean flutter; a single retrograde or hidden P wave means a junctional origin.", "competing diagnosis"],
   ["Focal atrial tachycardia",
    "Focal atrial tachycardia is a fast rhythm, over 100 and usually 150 to 250, from one ectopic atrial focus, with one consistently abnormal P wave before each QRS and a normal PR of 0.12 to 0.20 seconds. His rate is 46 and his P waves are inverted or buried. Tell: fast with a P before every QRS is atrial; slow with retrograde P waves is junctional.", "ruled out by a stated finding"]],
  C(62), "differential", "diagnosis"),

